vHARMONY

Discovery evidence gate
=======================

viSUM combines the sparse, threshold-qualified evidence emitted by every
enabled discovery program before running quality or taxonomic refinement. The
gate operates on normalized input contigs and does not reconcile taxonomy.

Calls made on extracted viral or proviral regions are credited to their input
contig. A contig advances when at least one enabled discovery program reports
qualified viral evidence. Cellular or plasmid evidence does not silently veto
a viral call; conflicts advance as ambiguous candidates so later stages can
resolve them. Contigs without viral evidence are withheld from refinement but
remain in the audit and noncandidate FASTA.

For each sample, the gate publishes under `discovery_gate/`:

  * `<prefix>.discovery_candidates.fasta` - full normalized contigs passed to
    quality/refinement stages such as CheckV.
  * `<prefix>.discovery_noncandidates.fasta` - all contigs not advanced.
  * `<prefix>.discovery_gate.tsv` - one decision row per input contig, including
    supporting and opposing tools.
  * `<prefix>.discovery_gate_summary.tsv` - sample-level routing counts.

The statuses are `viral` (two or more viral tools), `likely_viral` (one viral
tool), `ambiguous` (viral and cellular/plasmid evidence), `likely_nonviral`
(cellular/plasmid evidence only), and `unresolved` (no qualified evidence).
Only the first three advance. These labels route candidates; they are not the
pipeline's final viral-origin or taxonomy conclusions.

viCAT competitive architecture
==============================

When `--run_vicat true`, viSUM predicts ORFs once and searches them separately
against the viral MetaVR-derived database and the class-aware nonviral database
selected by `--vicat_nonviral_db`. Per ORF locus, the best viral and nonviral
hits compete by normalized bitscore using `--vicat_competitive_min_margin`.
The nonviral winner retains its class (`CELLULAR_CHROMOSOME`,
`CELLULAR_UNPLACED`, `PLASMID`, `PLASTID`, `MITOCHONDRIAL`, or
`SHARED_NONVIRAL`) in the viCAT evidence and final viHARMONY metadata.

An isolated viral-supported locus in cellular context is treated as cellular,
not as an extracted viral candidate. A spatial cluster of at least
`--vicat_cluster_min_viral_loci` viral-supported loci can flag a potential
provirus and pass the parent contig through the discovery gate for refinement.
Only `CELLULAR_CHROMOSOME` loci count as chromosome-flank support;
`CELLULAR_UNPLACED` remains valid evidence against a viral ORF but cannot be
used as a chromosome flank. viCAT cluster coordinates are advisory during
provirus refinement: they are recorded and can corroborate geNomad or CheckV,
but a viCAT-only boundary never trims a contig. Taxonomy for a refined provirus
is recalculated from viral-supported ORFs contained within the selected region.

CheckV database setup
=====================

CheckV is enabled as a post-discovery refinement tool for both DNA and RNA
candidate sequences. It runs only on sequences advanced by the discovery gate.
CheckV quality and viral/proviral features add support, while an undetermined
or absent quality estimate does not classify a candidate as nonviral.

With the defaults, viSUM downloads the current official CheckV database once
to `databases/checkv/checkv_db`, validates its genome, DIAMOND, and HMM assets,
and reuses it on later runs. A pre-existing database can be supplied with
`--checkv_db /absolute/path/to/checkv-db`. Supplying that option disables the
automatic download path and causes an incomplete database to fail with a clear
error. Use `--run_checkv false` to skip CheckV and its database setup.

Resource management
===================

viSUM separates local scheduler capacity from each program's task request.
Launch local runs through `./visum`: it applies `--max_cpus` before the Nextflow
JVM starts, making it an aggregate CPU scheduling cap. On Linux it also applies
CPU affinity to the complete viSUM process tree, preventing third-party tools
from using CPUs outside that cap. A direct `nextflow run` cannot do either and
treats `--max_cpus` only as a per-task ceiling.
`--max_memory` is also a per-task ceiling; a hard aggregate memory cap requires
an OS or cluster scheduler. Defaults are 8 logical CPUs and 48 GB per task.
Lightweight preparation, standardization, and gate processes use one CPU.

Each compute-heavy discovery program has an independent preferred CPU option.
Its effective task allocation is automatically capped at `--max_cpus`, so a
user may safely lower the total budget without overriding every tool:

  * `--genomad_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--virsorter2_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--ct3_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--deep6_cpus` (default 1)
  * `--deepmicroclass2_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--virbot_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--gianthunter_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--vicat_orf_cpus` (default 4)
  * `--vicat_cpus` (default 8; DIAMOND search)

The corresponding `*_memory` and `*_time` parameters in `visum.config` provide
per-task memory and runtime requests. Ordinary runs should normally change only
the total budget. For example, a shared-server run capped at eight CPUs uses:

    ./visum -profile local_safe \
      --max_cpus 8 --max_memory '48 GB' ...

The `local_full` profile starts with a 32-CPU/128-GB budget for a dedicated
machine. The separate `vicat_build` profile reserves 32 CPUs and 400 GB for the
optional, unusually large MetaVR5 database construction; it is not needed when
using an existing viCAT database.

The old `--threads`, `--vicat_threads`, `--vicat_orf_threads`, and
`--vicat_build_threads` options now fail with migration instructions. This is
intentional: accepting them silently would make it unclear whether they limit
one program or the entire workflow. CPU requests and the effective thread count
passed to each discovery tool are recorded in Nextflow's trace and tool run
metadata.

